F$6$Q\"1F'FapF+" }{TEXT 440 4 ".  \n" }{TEXT 440 1 "\n" }{TEXT 440 27 
"Write a procedure, call it " }{TEXT 409 3 "Ord" }{TEXT 440 12 ", such
 that " }{TEXT 410 8 "Ord(a,m)" }{TEXT 440 9 " returns " }{TEXT 411 4 
"FAIL" }{TEXT 440 46 " if the order does not exist and the order of " 
}{TEXT 412 10 "a modulo m" }{TEXT 440 17 " if  it  exists.\n" }{TEXT 
440 1 "\n" }{TEXT 413 91 "You may use the number theoretic result that
 the order of a modulo m exists if and only if " }{TEXT 414 13 "igcd(a
,m) = 1" }{TEXT 415 46 ". Also you may use the fact that the order of 
" }{TEXT 416 10 "a modulo m" }{TEXT 417 23 " is never greater than " }
{TEXT 418 5 "m - 1" }{TEXT 419 24 ". [Note that Maple uses " }{TEXT 
420 4 "igcd" }{TEXT 421 49 " for the greatest common divisor of intege
rs and " }{TEXT 422 3 "gcd" }{TEXT 423 46 " for greatest common diviso
r of polynomials.] " }{TEXT 440 1 "\n" }{TEXT 440 1 "\n" }{TEXT 424 4 
"(a) " }{TEXT 440 23 "Check that you obtain: " }{TEXT 425 1 "\n" }
{TEXT 426 1 "\n" }{TEXT 427 63 "        Ord(0,11) = FAIL,    Ord(2,11)
 = 10,     Ord(4,11) = 5." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 440 0 "" }}{PARA 0 
"" 0 "" {TEXT 428 3 "(b)" }{TEXT 440 9 " Compute " }{TEXT 429 10 "Ord(
a, 15)" }{TEXT 440 20 " for a from 0 to 20." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
207 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 207 11 "Problem 5. " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "
" {TEXT 440 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 440 15 "For each prime " }
{TEXT 430 1 "p" }{TEXT 440 50 " from 2 to 100 find the smallest positi
ve integer " }{TEXT 431 1 "a" }{TEXT 440 11 " such that " }{TEXT 432 
14 "Ord(a,p) = p-1" }{TEXT 440 25 ". Print out a table with " }{TEXT 
433 4 "p, a" }{TEXT 440 15 " on each row. \n" }{TEXT 440 1 "\n" }{TEXT
 440 23 "[Such an a is called a " }{TEXT 434 27 "primitive elements mo
dulo p" }{TEXT 440 47 " and plays an important role in number theory.]
" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 440 1 " " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 435 5 "Hin
t:" }{TEXT 440 82 " You might use a loop for p from 2 to 100. If p is \+
not prime then use the command " }{TEXT 436 5 "next;" }{TEXT 440 195 "
 to go to the next value of p. If p is prime then go into another loop
 for a from 1 to p - 1 looking for an a which has order 1 modulo p. Wh
en you find one, print it out and then use the command " }{TEXT 437 6 
"break;" }{TEXT 440 20 " to leave the loop.\n" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT
 440 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 210 "" 0 "" {TEXT 447 8 "- End - " }}}
